Overloading vs Overriding in Java

In Java, overloading and overriding are two important concepts that are used to achieve polymorphism. Overloading is a compile-time polymorphism, while overriding is a runtime polymorphism.

Overloading

Overloading is the process of defining multiple methods with the same name but different parameters in the same class. The parameters can differ in type, number, or order. The method that is called depends on the arguments passed to it.

Example:

public class Overloading {
public void display(int a) {
System.out.println("Integer: " + a);
}
public void display(double a) {
System.out.println("Double: " + a);
}
public void display(String a) {
System.out.println("String: " + a);
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
Overloading obj = new Overloading();
obj.display(10); // Integer: 10
obj.display(10.5); // Double: 10.5
obj.display("Hello"); // String: Hello
}
}

